stxlib::glyphs, the font reader and the hinter

The font reader of stxlib::glyphs (include/stxlib/font_file.hpp and
src/algorithms/glyphs/font_file.cpp, font_truetype.cpp, font_cff.cpp,
font_reader.hpp) and its hinter (include/stxlib/glyph_hinter.hpp and
src/algorithms/glyphs/hinter.hpp, hinter.cpp, hinter_outline.cpp,
hinter_segments.cpp, hinter_edges.cpp, hinter_metrics.cpp, hinter_fit.cpp,
hinter_align.cpp, hinter_accents.cpp, hinter_tables.inc, hinter_adjust.inc)
are part of STXlib and are STXlib's own code, under STXlib's licence (the
repository's LICENSE). They are a conversion to house-style C++ of the
design of three projects. Two are each offered under the MIT licence or the
Apache License 2.0; what is after them is taken under the MIT licence, and
carries their notices, which follow unchanged.

- ttf-parser 0.25.1 (https://github.com/harfbuzz/ttf-parser), by Yevhenii
  Reizner: the table directory and collections, the face's numbers, the
  character maps, advances, a glyph's range in glyf and the two passes over
  a simple glyph's flags and coordinates, the indexes and dictionaries of
  CFF and the operators of its charstrings.
- Skrifa 0.50.0 and read-fonts 0.47.0 (https://github.com/googlefonts/
  fontations), by the Fontations developers: a composite glyph put together
  in whole numbers, the outline placed by its left side bearing, a scaled
  glyph's parts scaled on their own, and a CFF glyph's coordinates in whole
  font units with the lines and points that are left out; and the whole of
  the hinter: Skrifa's auto-hinter (src/outline/autohint), with its table of
  scripts, styles, blue zone characters and character ranges
  (generated/generated_autohint_styles.rs), from which hinter_tables.inc is
  made by tools/gen_hinter_tables.py.

The third is the FreeType Project: part of the hinter is a conversion of
its own source, taken under the FreeType Project License, which follows
unchanged at the end of this file:

- FreeType 2.14.2 (https://freetype.org), src/autofit/afadjust.c and the
  parts of src/autofit/aflatin.c that use it, by David Turner, Robert
  Wilhelm and Werner Lemberg, written by Craig White: the care for accents
  (hinter_accents.cpp: a tilde kept tall enough, an accent's edges kept off
  the blue zones, an accent moved a pixel clear of its letter), the check
  in hinter_fit.cpp that a serif edge is a real serif, and the table of
  accented characters and of the blue zones that are letters' bottoms
  (hinter_adjust.inc, made by tools/gen_hinter_adjust.py from afadjust.c
  and afblue.dat). The code is rewritten in STXlib's house style and for
  STXlib's data; none of FreeType's files is in STXlib.

Skrifa's auto-hinter itself follows the auto-hinter of the FreeType Project
(the autofit module) place by place, as its source says at each, and its
table is made from that module's.

